  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{2x-6}{{x}^{2}-7x+12}.$",
  "worked_solution": "We start by factoring the function as much as possible and canceling the common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x-6}{{x}^{2}-7x+12} & =\\frac{2x-6}{{x}^{2}-4x-3x+12} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x-3)}{x(x-4)-3(x-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x-3)}{(x-4)(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cancel{(x-3)}}{(x-4)\\cancel{(x-3)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx-4=0\\,\u27f9\\,x=4.\n$$\n\nSo the *only* vertical asymptote is at $x=4.$",
